{"purpose":"Record that eka-cli vendors copies of standard artifacts with no integrity link to the standard repository, so a silent divergence can ship, and state the acceptance criteria.","content":"## 1. Situation\neka-cli embeds vendored artifacts from eka-standard:\n\n```go\n//go:embed EKA                          standardembed.go\n//go:embed templates/hooks/pre-commit   hooksembed.go\n//go:embed templates/hooks/pre-push     hooksembed.go\n```\n\nAt review time both copies are byte-identical to their eka-standard counterparts (verified by diff). Nothing enforces that they stay so.\n\n## 2. Why tests do not catch drift\nThe existing test asserts *internal* consistency: the embedded declaration parses and its version line matches `exchange.SpecificationVersion`. A hand-edited `EKAv2` that keeps a valid `Version 1.3` line satisfies every current assertion while its content diverges from the published standard. No hash, manifest, or provenance record links the vendored bytes to a standard release.\n\n`scripts/platform-sync.sh` prefers a sibling `eka-standard` checkout and falls back to the vendored copy, which makes the vendored copy a live fallback path rather than dead weight, and therefore a live drift risk. Nothing in the repository refreshes the vendored `EKAv2`.\n\n## 3. Secondary case\nThe hook templates are duplicated with zero tests on either side. A behaviour change to a hook in eka-standard silently does not reach repositories scaffolding from eka-cli.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria\n- The vendored standard artifacts carry a recorded expected digest of the eka-standard release they came from.\n- A test fails when the vendored bytes differ from the recorded digest, naming the standard release the digests correspond to.\n- Refreshing the vendored copy is a repeatable scripted step, not a manual copy, and it updates the recorded digests in the same operation.\n- Hook templates are covered by at least one assertion that their behaviour matches the standard's copies."}